From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 04:07:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: give each timestamp of a compute encoder its own
 sample

A timestamp whose stage has no render stage of its own is sampled by a
compute encoder, always into entry 0 of the pool's counter heap, and
read back when the encoder stops. Samples taken by one encoder overwrite
each other, so every query of the encoder reads the time of the last
one. When commands lie between the writes, a query then reports a time
after commands recorded later than it, which vkCmdWriteTimestamp2 does
not allow: it latches the timer when all previous commands have
completed the stage. A top-of-pipe write, a copy, a bottom-of-pipe
write, a second copy and a last top-of-pipe write, all between two
passes, read one time.

The encoder that runs ahead of the render encoder takes the top stages.
It now stops before a second sample into a heap it has not resolved yet,
and the next encoder samples entry 0 again. Outside a started pass that
opens no command buffer: the encoder runs in order in the main one.
Inside a started pass the encoder has a command buffer of its own, so
each further top-stage timestamp into the same pool ends one command
buffer and opens another. The encoder that runs after the render encoder
takes the bottom stages inside a pass, and it cannot stop while the
pass's render encoder is open: its command buffer would be submitted
ahead of the pass, with the pass's post-render work in it. Each of its
samples takes the next entry, which a heap has for every query of its
pool; more samples into one heap than the pool has queries share the
last entry. Only samples into a heap count: a pipeline-statistics
publication takes none.

The transform feedback stage is at the end of a pass, like bottom of
pipe, because a draw's capture is done once the draws before it have
run. A transform feedback timestamp written after a capture of the same
pass was sampled by the encoder ahead of the pass, before that capture
had run, and read the same time as the top-of-pipe write before it; it
now follows the pass's draws. Writes with no command between them may
read alike or either way round under Vulkan's rule. The encoders keep
their recording order all the same, and the test checks that.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c | 10 +++++++
 src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.h |  1 +
 src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_query_pool.c | 40 ++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 3 files changed, 49 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c
index bbe6df540b4edc5ace683dd9b078dd9b06fbf437..8390f70828acc23163e8924530cde655bb20980a 100644
--- a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c
@@ -605,6 +605,16 @@ kk_stop_encoder(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd, struct kk_encoder_state *es)
    es->cmd_buf = NULL;
 }
 
+/* Stops the compute encoder that runs ahead of the render encoder, or in
+ * recording order when none is open, so that it resolves the timestamps it
+ * sampled. The next compute starts another. */
+void
+cs_stop_pre_compute(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd)
+{
+   if (cmd->pre_gfx->encoder)
+      kk_stop_encoder(cmd, cmd->pre_gfx);
+}
+
 void
 cs_end(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd)
 {
diff --git a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.h b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.h
index af62d43c89cfbd9ca95503ec6dae7ad435a3b223..65aa82c7d8b0798c393a709f5fa5a1f0485543a4 100644
--- a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.h
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.h
@@ -481,6 +481,7 @@ void kk_cmd_buffer_set_vertex_amplification(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd,
                                             const uint32_t *layer_ids,
                                             uint32_t count);
 mtl_compute_encoder *cs_get_compute(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd, bool pre_gfx);
+void cs_stop_pre_compute(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd);
 void cs_end(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd);
 void cs_flush(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd);
 /* Re-emits the graphics state of the draw being recorded into a render
diff --git a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_query_pool.c b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_query_pool.c
index b8abb9fefcbd59ac4e0d183a304f5b798561fe62..6fb8e2b78ae7896e19d4dab89ee88ec17fd8e203 100644
--- a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_query_pool.c
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_query_pool.c
@@ -349,6 +349,21 @@ kk_pipeline_stages_to_mtl_render_stage(VkPipelineStageFlags2 vk_flags)
    return 0;
 }
 
+/* The samples the compute encoder `es` has taken into `heap` since it began,
+ * which it has not yet resolved. A pipeline-statistics publication takes no
+ * sample and has no heap. */
+static uint32_t
+kk_encoder_samples_into(const struct kk_encoder_state *es,
+                        const mtl_counter_heap *heap)
+{
+   uint32_t samples = 0;
+   util_dynarray_foreach(&es->ts_resolves, struct kk_ts_resolve, r) {
+      if (r->heap == heap)
+         samples++;
+   }
+   return samples;
+}
+
 VKAPI_ATTR void VKAPI_CALL
 kk_CmdWriteTimestamp2(VkCommandBuffer commandBuffer,
                       VkPipelineStageFlags2 stage, VkQueryPool queryPool,
@@ -411,19 +426,40 @@ kk_CmdWriteTimestamp2(VkCommandBuffer commandBuffer,
       bool top = true;
       struct kk_encoder_state *es;
 
+      /* Stages with no render stage of their own. Inside a render pass,
+       * those at its end sample after the pass's draws; the others sample
+       * before it. The transform feedback stage is at the end: a draw's
+       * capture is done by the time the draws before it have run. */
       const VkPipelineStageFlagBits2 bottom_mask =
-         VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_2_BOTTOM_OF_PIPE_BIT;
+         VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_2_BOTTOM_OF_PIPE_BIT |
+         VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_2_TRANSFORM_FEEDBACK_BIT_EXT;
       if (cmd->gfx.encoder && ((stage & bottom_mask) != 0)) {
          top = false;
       }
       es = top ? cmd->pre_gfx : cmd->post_gfx;
 
+      /* An encoder resolves its samples when it stops, each from the heap
+       * entry it was sampled into. A second sample into an entry would
+       * overwrite the first, and both queries would read the later time. The
+       * pre-render encoder stops before such a sample and a new one takes it.
+       * The post-render encoder cannot stop while the pass's render encoder
+       * is open, because its command buffer would then run ahead of the pass,
+       * so each of its samples takes the next entry; the entries are
+       * adjacent from the first, and a heap has an entry for each query. */
+      uint32_t entry = kk_encoder_samples_into(es, pool->ts.heap);
+      if (entry != 0 && top) {
+         cs_stop_pre_compute(cmd);
+         entry = 0;
+      }
+      entry = MIN2(entry, pool->vk.query_count - 1);
+      resolve.index = entry;
+
       /* Keep compute work to trigger sampling even for timestamp-only
        * command buffers, without prematurely publishing availability. */
       libkk_write_u32(cmd, kk_grid_1d(1), top,
                       kk_query_available_addr(pool, query), false);
 
-      mtl_compute_write_timestamp(es->encoder, pool->ts.heap, 0);
+      mtl_compute_write_timestamp(es->encoder, pool->ts.heap, entry);
       mtl_retain(resolve.heap);
       mtl_retain(resolve.availability);
       util_dynarray_append(&es->ts_resolves, resolve);
